\ProvidesPackage{PassageMeta}[2026/08/25 Greppable passage metadata (stores title/label)]

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
% WHY THIS EXISTS
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
% The passage bank is one passage per file, shared across courses and pulled into
% prose documents with \importpassage. This block is what a script can read off
% disk WITHOUT running LaTeX -- the same premise as ProblemMeta and FactMeta,
% though it ends up serving a different purpose here (see NO SEARCH KEYS below):
% what find-passages does with it is compute the relative directory for an import
% line and list what a passage will need from the document taking it.
%
%   \PassageMeta{
%     title    = {How We Mark},
%     label    = {sec:howwemark},
%     requires = {tikz},
%   }
%
% Until 2026-08-25 the header comment at the top of each passage WAS the index --
% sufficient at single digits, and TODO.md predicted it would stop being so
% somewhere in the teens. It stopped at eleven, when the bank got a picker.
%
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
% HOW THIS DIFFERS FROM ITS TWO SIBLINGS
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
% THIS ONE STORES, and it is the only one that does. ProblemMeta and FactMeta
% discard every value: the handlers are empty and the block exists so that a
% mistyped key errors at compile time. Here `title' and `label' have REAL
% handlers, because a passage differs from a fact in WHERE ITS NAME IS CONSUMED.
%
% A fact's `name' is consumed at the IMPORT SITE -- \importfact[name=...] titles
% the box -- so find-facts injects it from the block into the call site and
% FactMeta itself never needs the value. A passage's title is consumed INSIDE THE
% PASSAGE FILE, by \PassageTitle, a few lines below this block. There is no import
% site to inject it into. So either this package stores it, or the same string is
% written twice in one file, four lines apart -- and one job under two spellings
% is exactly the drift that cost `desc'/`name' a rename on 2026-08-13.
%
% Store-only was never a principle anyway, only a consequence: ProblemMeta says so
% itself, recording that it USED to print metadata in the margin and how to bring
% that back. This is the first block where something needs the values again.
%
% THE PRICE IS REMOVABILITY, and it is worth naming. FactMeta can be deleted and
% every fact still builds. Delete this one and every passage loses its heading. So
% \PassageTitle does not degrade quietly: with no title it raises a ClassError
% naming the file, in the same voice as the too-deep-heading error beside it.
% This is the one metadata package the toolkit cannot do without.
%
% NO `level' KEY, deliberately, though an early sketch of this schema had one.
% `level' says where a passage's natural depth LANDS in the document importing it,
% which makes it a property of that document and not of the passage -- so it
% cannot be metadata, and \importpassage keeps taking it at the call site.
% Consequence: a passage has NO injectable keys at all, so find-passages takes no
% --with. It is the only kind of which that is true.
%
% NO `depth' KEY EITHER, and that one was tried and removed. The idea was to
% record how far a passage reaches below its own title, so you could tell before
% shifting whether level=subsection was legal. It records nothing: a shift maps
% section->subsection and subsection->subsubsection, the class styles three ranks,
% so a title plus one rank of subsections still fits -- verified by importing the
% deepest passage in the bank at level=subsection and getting clean 1.1.1's. The
% limit only bites a passage carrying \subsubsection itself, which none does, and
% CourseDocument already refuses that at build time with a message naming the fix.
% A key duplicating a check the compiler performs is a second reader of one fact.
%
% NO `topics' EITHER. A passage's filename is its title (why-learn-math.tex), so
% topics restated what the picker could already fuzzy-match; and in the bank as
% tagged, `marking' covered eight of eleven files while eight other values covered
% one file each -- and `--topic marking' selected exactly the same eight files as
% `--audience' did. One partition does not need two keys. Problems earn topics
% because a problem's filename says nothing about what it tests.
%
% AND NO `audience', which went last and took the last filter with it. It had no
% build-time effect; its --audience filter duplicated what the picker already does
% (fzf matches the displayed columns, so typing `students' narrows without it);
% and it restated the import graph, since every passage is imported by exactly one
% document and the single-valued tags were one-to-one with which. The multi-valued
% ones were worse than redundant: mostly-right-impression and
% serious-and-small-errors were tagged for students but appear only in the markers'
% manual, so the tag recorded that ANOTHER document holds similar prose -- not a
% fact about the file. Re-add it if a passage is ever imported by two documents;
% that is the situation it was invented for and the situation that does not exist.
%
% What is left is a block with NO SEARCH KEYS AT ALL, which is the honest shape:
% two values the LaTeX needs, and one warning. Finding things was never this
% bank's problem -- computing ../teaching/passages/ was, and --import --from does
% that.
%
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
% THE KEYS
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
% title     the passage's own title, typeset by \PassageTitle. Required: a
%           passage with no title has no heading, and that is an error.
% label     the \label \PassageTitle attaches, so \secref can reach the passage.
%           It names the passage's own idea and therefore belongs to the passage,
%           not to whichever document imports it. Optional -- omit it for a
%           passage nothing refers to. Prefix `sec:', like every other sectioning
%           label in the courses.
% requires  what the IMPORTING DOCUMENT must supply or the build fails: course
%           macros (\MarkerCount) and packages (tikz) alike, because from the
%           author's side those are one question -- what must be in place before
%           this passage will compile? -- even though the fixes differ (a
%           courseinfo definition vs a \usepackage). The leading backslash is the
%           discriminator, and is why --vocab can tell the two species apart.
%           The one key with no analogue in the other two banks, and the reason a
%           passage can fail in a way a problem cannot. Absent means self-contained;
%           write nothing rather than `requires = {}'.
%
% Keep ONE KEY PER LINE, values braced. find-passages parses the block textually,
% line by line; reflowing it onto a single line still compiles fine but makes the
% passage invisible to the script. That is the one way to get the two readers out
% of step, so it is worth the discipline.
%
% NB `#' is special to LaTeX, so a value containing one must write it \#.
% find-passages knows this and reports it back without the backslash. A macro name
% in `requires' needs no such care: the handler never expands it, so \MarkerCount
% is stored and dropped even in a document that has never defined it.

\RequirePackage{keyval}

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
% THE TWO STORED VALUES
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
% Read by \PassageTitle in CourseDocument.cls -- the only reader. The setter owns
% the name, so they are PassageMeta@ and the class only looks at them; the class
% \providecommand's them too, so a document that somehow lacks this package gets
% the ClassError rather than an undefined control sequence.
%
% PLAIN \def, NOT \gdef, and that is load-bearing. \importpassage wraps its
% \import in \begingroup...\endgroup, and this block is read inside that group, so
% a local definition dies with the import that set it. Per-import isolation comes
% free and a \gdef would defeat it -- the second passage in a document would
% inherit the first one's title wherever its own block omitted one.
%
% Deferred writes are safe under that scoping: \section and \label both route
% through \protected@write, which \edef's its argument at the point of the call --
% inside the group, while the value still exists -- and only defers the \write
% itself. The .toc and .aux therefore see the text, not a dead macro.
\newcommand{\PassageMeta@title}{}
\newcommand{\PassageMeta@label}{}

% Cleared at the top of every block rather than trusting the caller's group, so a
% passage \input directly (no \importpassage, hence no group) still cannot inherit
% a previous one's title.
\define@key{PassageMeta}{title}   {\def\PassageMeta@title{#1}}
\define@key{PassageMeta}{label}   {\def\PassageMeta@label{#1}}

% Accepted, validated, DISCARDED -- the sibling pattern, and down to a single key.
% A mistyped `require' for `requires' errors at compile time; the value itself is
% read off disk by find-passages, never from here.
\define@key{PassageMeta}{requires}{}

\newcommand{\PassageMeta}[1]{%
  \def\PassageMeta@title{}%
  \def\PassageMeta@label{}%
  \setkeys{PassageMeta}{#1}\ignorespaces
}
